{
  "abstract": "For every real number ξ > 0, the sequence of integer parts ⌊ξ(7/5)^n⌋, n = 0, 1, 2, …, contains infinitely many composite terms. The Lean proof establishes the stronger statement that infinitely many terms share a nontrivial divisor with 4290 = 2·3·5·11·13, by a finite graph certificate: an orbit avoiding these five primes would give an infinite walk in a finite labelled graph whose vertices record a residue modulo 4290 and a cell of the fractional part, refined in four stages from 32 to 2048 cells, and every cyclic strongly connected component of that graph is excluded by a rank and phase certificate together with an elementary lemma that the carry sequence of such an orbit is not eventually periodic. The finite certificate is checked inside the Lean kernel (`decide +kernel`); no `native_decide` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` are used.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Yuri Odagiri"
    }
  ],
